# 🌐 5. HTTP Methods

HTTP methods define **what action the client wants to perform** on a resource.

| Method | Purpose | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `GET` | Retrieve data | `/products/` |
| `POST` | Create new data | `/products/` |
| `PUT` | Replace/update entire resource | `/products/10/` |
| `PATCH` | Partially update resource | `/products/10/` |
| `DELETE` | Delete data | `/products/10/` |

---

## 🟢 GET — Retrieve Data

Used to retrieve existing data.

```http
GET /api/products/
```

Example response:

```json
[
    {
        "id": 1,
        "name": "Laptop"
    },
    {
        "id": 2,
        "name": "Phone"
    }
]
```

### Example

```text
GET /api/products/
        ↓
Get all products
```

---

## 🔵 POST — Create Data

Used to create a new resource.

```http
POST /api/products/
```

Request body:

```json
{
    "name": "Keyboard",
    "price": 2500
}
```

The server creates a new product.

```text
POST
 ↓
Create new product
```

---

## 🟡 PUT — Update Entire Resource

Used to **replace or completely update** a resource.

```http
PUT /api/products/10/
```

Request:

```json
{
    "name": "Gaming Keyboard",
    "price": 3000
}
```

Conceptually:

```text
Existing Resource
        ↓
    PUT Request
        ↓
Replaced/Updated Resource
```

---

## 🟠 PATCH — Partial Update

Used when only some fields need to be updated.

```http
PATCH /api/products/10/
```

Request:

```json
{
    "price": 2800
}
```

Only the specified field is changed.

```text
Existing Product
       ↓
PATCH price
       ↓
Updated Product
```

### PUT vs PATCH

```text
PUT
 ↓
Complete replacement/update

PATCH
 ↓
Partial update
```

---

## 🔴 DELETE — Remove Data

Used to delete a resource.

```http
DELETE /api/products/10/
```

Flow:

```text
DELETE /api/products/10/
            ↓
       Find Product
            ↓
          Delete
            ↓
       Send Response
```

